{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is warning others not to make purchases in this casino slots game. In a slots game, the thing you buy is the game's coins/chips. The reviewer reports that 161 billion coins vanished and that the developer attributed the disappearance to 'display issues' — a technical fault. The loss of coins is therefore being explained as a malfunction, not as a deliberate design rule, so the disappearance itself does not support a dark-pattern label under R11. What remains is the purchase mechanism itself: real money converted into the in-game currency (coins). That exchange is implied by 'Do not buy from this game' alongside 'missing coins lost of 161Billion.'",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "Do not buy from this game!!! Constant \"display\" issues as the developer states when you tell them you are missing coins lost of 161Billion.",
      "rule_applied": "Intermediate Currency definition / boundary rule vs Purchases denominated in currency",
      "rationale": "The warning 'Do not buy' together with 'missing coins' implies the reviewer purchased the in-game currency (coins) with real money, satisfying the real-money-to-virtual-currency exchange; the direction is money -> coins."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a bonus 'genie game' inside a casino slots app. They describe a wheel that spins, appears to stop on a great prize (100 million with a 5x multiplier), but then the genie moves the wheel to a 'spin tag' and the resulting prize is much lower (48 million). This is a claim that the game manipulates or rigs the outcome of the wheel after it has appeared to land on a good prize. The closing request for a 'player choice to keep or spin' is a suggestion for a feature that does not exist, so it does not itself trigger a label. The wheel is a chance mechanic, but no monetary stake or real-money currency purchase is described in this span, so Gambling and Intermediate Currency do not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price, from 100 million with 5x multiplier to a 48 million",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Alleged rigging: 'Assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes.'",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes the wheel appearing to land on a great prize and then being moved by the genie to yield a lower prize, asserting the outcome is manipulated rather than left to genuine chance."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer makes three observations. First, there is a 30-second wait to connect to a match, which is a matchmaking/queue delay rather than a designed in-game timer gate (no energy, build timer, or cooldown is described). Second, the game presents rewards that feel substantial but the reviewer says they are mostly for show, which is a complaint about reward value, not about how rewards are delivered. Third, obtaining weapon upgrades or perks is difficult and requires the player to keep battling to win, naming the repeated activity through which progress is earned. No spending or payment alternative is mentioned anywhere in the review.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Its difficult to get weapon upgrades or any percs others you stick battling to win",
      "rule_applied": "definition / boundary rule vs Slow Progress",
      "rationale": "The span names the repeated activity (battling to win) that the player must keep doing in order to obtain weapon upgrades and perks, so progress is earned through repetitive play rather than merely described as slow."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes Clash Royale's ranked ladder: after reaching 8k trophies, the player only faces opponents using particular powerful cards (Mega Knight, PEKKA, Golem) and finds defending against spammed versions of them frustrating. The reviewer calls the game pay to win and says skill alone is not enough to climb, with each update making it more pay to win. The complaint about specific cards being buffed is a balance/meta complaint rather than a described dark-pattern mechanic. The trophy ladder and playing against other players satisfy the Competition pattern, and the explicit pay-to-win claim satisfies Pay to Progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes a competitive trophy ladder where they are matched against other players."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that spending gives an advantage and that skill alone does not allow climbing, which is the Pay to Win instance of Pay to Progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review begins with a customer-service dispute: a required game element did not appear within the allotted original and additional moves, and support would not refund 9 diamonds. That complaint describes a support interaction rather than a clearly identified dark pattern. The reviewer then says the game is not pay-to-win but would only buy the pass, which a web search confirms is Fishdom's purchasable seasonal pass. Finally, the reviewer advises getting friends and a team and trading lives, which names an existing reciprocal resource-exchange feature.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "don't buy from this game beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes indicator; boundary rule vs Playing by Appointment states a paid pass or subscription alone is Recurring Fee.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer identifies the pass as the purchasable seasonal-pass product, which is a recurring-fee battle-pass mechanic."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Get friends and a team and trade lives",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Forced Fellowship: exchanging resources or lives with people already playing is Reciprocity.",
      "rationale": "Trading lives among friends and team describes an existing reciprocal resource-transfer mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "what is the pass in Fishdom game",
  "search_result": "Fishdom offers a purchasable monthly Season Pass that provides exclusive rewards and challenges and ends after the event concludes."
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is mostly positive and frames their complaints as suggestions, but the suggestions reveal existing mechanics in the game. First, lives regenerate on a timer and the player wishes new lives came every 5-10 minutes, which implies the current regeneration wait is longer than that. Second, the game presents many small renovation tasks such as celebrating, which the player describes as unnecessary. Third, daily prizes reset when a day is missed, which the player wishes did not happen. Under R12, the proposed changes do not get tagged, but the existing behaviours they describe alongside the suggestions remain codable.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play (low-level): in-game timers that make you arbitrarily wait for something; lives/energy refilling over time",
      "rationale": "The suggestion implies lives currently regenerate on a timer longer than 5-10 minutes, which is a life refill wait the game imposes."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "not as many small and unnecessary tasks like celebrating",
      "rule_applied": "definition: performing repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress",
      "rationale": "The reviewer names the task type (celebrating) and describes the tasks as small and unnecessary, implying the game requires many such tedious tasks to progress."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "definition: daily log-in reward with a penalty for missing a day (streak reset)",
      "rationale": "Daily prizes exist and resetting them after a missed day is a penalty for not logging in daily, which is Daily Rewards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing an in-game ad that cannot be closed: after the ad plays there is no close button, tapping anywhere on the screen routes the player to the store, returning to the game leaves the ad screen stuck, and the only way out is to force-quit the app. Three mechanics are present at once. The ad itself is compulsory viewing with no skip or close (Advertisement). The interface removes the exit control entirely and makes any tap select the store instead of dismissing the ad (UI Misdirection's obstructed-opt-out arm). And the player is directed to the purchase screen as a default action with no way to decline or dismiss it (Easy to Purchase's Low Barrier / Prompted-to-Buy behaviour). No web search is needed because the mechanics are described directly.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer is compelled to view an ad with no way to close it, which is forced ad viewing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store",
      "rule_applied": "definition, second arm: obstructing an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The close control is absent and any tap routes the player to the store, obstructing the opt-out from the ad and the purchase surface."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store",
      "rule_applied": "Low Barrier / Default To Purchase",
      "rationale": "The player is directed to the store as a default action with no way to opt out, which reduces friction before spending."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
