  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Pay to Skip indicator and definition",
      "rationale": "The tasks are described as impossible to complete without purchasing boosters, making payment the route to progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a time-limited in-game event with a 525-dice reward. They were three tasks short of the reward, ran out of the dice resource needed to continue, and the event UI stated two days remained. After a real-world delay (sleeping), the event had changed or ended, and the reward was lost. The core mechanic described is the game's clock operating on the player: a reward tied to a deadline, forfeited when not completed within the game-defined window. The reviewer also mentions never purchasing due to distrust of the F2P model, but no paid alternative, paywall, or currency conversion is actually described. The 'waste of time' and 'low blow' statements are evaluation, not mechanics. No web search is needed; the terms (dice, event rewards) are clear in context.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "The latest event I was about to get 525 dice as reward, I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Fear of missing out: where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.",
      "rationale": "The event imposes a game-defined deadline, and the 525-dice reward is forfeited because the event changed before the stated time, showing the player is made to play on the game's schedule rather than their own."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes three distinct things after the new season started. First, friend invitations were previously tied to rewards and now are not credited: the review names inviting friends and getting them to join Facebook as something the player did in expectation of rewards, which is a social recruitment incentive. Second, the player describes struggling to get dice and then a losing run at max bet with no shutdowns or bank heists; this is a chance-based outcome whose stake is earned dice, and no real-money purchase is mentioned anywhere in the review, so the gambling/monetary labels do not reach it, nor does a bare losing run code Illusion of Control. Third, sticker packs keep yielding duplicates, which shows both a collection-completion drive and randomized, unwanted drops from the sticker pool. Finally, the inability to log in due to an internet connection error is a technical fault and is suppressed under R11, not a design pattern.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "I get no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme",
      "rationale": "The player invited friends to join and expected rewards, describing the game's incentive to bring new people into its social circle even though the reward stopped being credited."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player is working toward completing sticker sets and is blocked by receiving only duplicates, which is the collection-completion compulsion."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomised pool (Reward Mania boundary vs Reward described only by its value)",
      "rationale": "The sticker packs deliver randomized duplicates rather than the missing items, which is a variable-reward delivery mechanic rather than a complaint about reward value."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a UI dark pattern in the bet-table selection. On opening the game or the 1v1 section, the interface automatically scrolls to and selects the highest possible bet table based on the player's coin balance, which the reviewer says causes accidental mis-clicks. Those mis-clicks lose the player's hard-earned coins, and the player is then forced to buy coins with real money to keep playing. Three mechanics are present: the interface steering the player toward the highest bet (attention-direction against the player's intent), the loss of coins forcing a real-money coin purchase to continue, and the conversion of real money into in-game coins ('real ones' clearly refers to coins as opposed to 'hard-earned' coins).",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "definition (attention-directing arm)",
      "rationale": "The interface steers the player to the highest-stakes option by default, directing attention/selection away from the player's intended choice and toward a costly option."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Losing all coins blocks further play, and the player is forced to buy coins with real money to continue, which is payment required to progress/keep playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency / boundary rule (currency as the object acquired)",
      "rationale": "'Real ones' refers to coins purchased with real money as opposed to hard-earned coins, showing real money being converted into in-game currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing how the game becomes progressively less rewarding the further they get. They make two concrete claims about the design. First, they assert the RNG is biased against the player, i.e., outcomes are not fair or random but tuned against them. Second, they describe the economy: playing without spending real money is unrewarding, and by level 100 a level costs 2200 in-game currency while winning barely returns that amount, so the free path does not sustain continued play and pushes spending. There is no scheduling, social, or chance-stake mechanic described. The 'rewards need a revamp' line is a complaint about reward value, not about how rewards are delivered, so it does not trigger Reward Mania.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you it's crazy",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Alleged rigging: assign where the span asserts the game manipulates the determination of outcomes, a non-random deal or a fixed win ratio.",
      "rationale": "The span names a mechanism (the RNG) and asserts it is deliberately biased against the player rather than fair or random, which is a claim of outcome manipulation."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's not rewarding playing the game without spending actual money.. at around level 100 it costs 2200 but if you win the level you'll barely make that back",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Free alternative: the free route falls short of smooth advancement, forcing spending.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer contrasts playing without spending against a paid route and shows the free economy drains currency (cost 2200, reward barely covers it), so progress is not self-sustaining without money."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

